{"reason": "The current model overfits (train loss ~0.33, val loss rises after epoch 3-4, best val log loss ~0.674). The initial plan's core improvements (multi-head attention pooling, OneCycleLR scheduler, differential learning rates, increased label smoothing) address the root cause: mean+max pooling dilutes stylistic cues. Historical experiments confirm that attention mechanisms improve generalization on author attribution, and OneCycleLR with differential LR prevents overfitting. Model architecture and training strategy changes are most promising and directly implementable without altering the feature extraction pipeline or data split.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current mean+max pooling of BiLSTM outputs with a multi-head self-attention pooling mechanism. Add a MultiheadAttention layer (4 heads, embed_dim=hidden_dim*2) after the BiLSTM; use the learned attention to compute a weighted sum of hidden states across time steps. Concatenate the weighted sum with an additional max-pooled vector for final LSTM output. Keep the BiLSTM output dimension (hidden_dim*2) and char CNN pipeline unchanged. This allows the model to focus on position-dependent stylistic markers (e.g., rare word usage, sentence-initial conjunctions) as evidenced by successful similar experiments on author attribution.", "training_evaluation": "Replace CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with OneCycleLR scheduler (max_lr=1e-3, pct_start=0.3, div_factor=25, final_div_factor=1e4) and apply differential learning rates: embedding layers at 0.33x (≈3.3e-4), BiLSTM+char convs at 1x (1e-3), feature projection+classifier at 3x (3e-3). Increase label_smoothing from 0.1 to 0.2. These changes are directly inspired by historical successful experiments where OneCycleLR with aggressive annealing and higher label smoothing prevented overfitting and improved generalization by converging to wider minima."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current model overfits (train loss ~0.33, val loss rises after epoch 3-4, best val log loss ~0.674). The initial plan's core improvements (multi-head attention pooling, OneCycleLR scheduler, differential learning rates, increased label smoothing) address the root cause: mean+max pooling dilutes stylistic cues. Historical experiments confirm that attention mechanisms improve generalization on author attribution, and OneCycleLR with differential LR prevents overfitting. Model architecture and training strategy changes are most promising and directly implementable without altering the feature extraction pipeline or data split.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current mean+max pooling of BiLSTM outputs with a multi-head self-attention pooling mechanism. Add a MultiheadAttention layer (4 heads, embed_dim=hidden_dim*2) after the BiLSTM; use the learned attention to compute a weighted sum of hidden states across time steps. Concatenate the weighted sum with an additional max-pooled vector for final LSTM output. Keep the BiLSTM output dimension (hidden_dim*2) and char CNN pipeline unchanged. This allows the model to focus on position-dependent stylistic markers (e.g., rare word usage, sentence-initial conjunctions) as evidenced by successful similar experiments on author attribution.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with OneCycleLR scheduler (max_lr=1e-3, pct_start=0.3, div_factor=25, final_div_factor=1e4) and apply differential learning rates: embedding layers at 0.33x (≈3.3e-4), BiLSTM+char convs at 1x (1e-3), feature projection+classifier at 3x (3e-3). Increase label_smoothing from 0.1 to 0.2. These changes are directly inspired by historical successful experiments where OneCycleLR with aggressive annealing and higher label smoothing prevented overfitting and improved generalization by converging to wider minima."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show validation log-loss plateauing around 0.41-0.43 with a training-validation gap of ~0.10, confirming the initial plan's diagnosis that engineered features are under-utilized. The initial plan's cross-attention mechanism and curriculum learning are promising but need refinement: historical success with multi-head attention on BiLSTM suggests attention works for author attribution, but failed experiments show OneCycleLR combined with high label smoothing can be unstable—here we use gradient accumulation and decaying label smoothing for stability. Model architecture change addresses the 64/832 dimension bottleneck directly, and training strategy changes ensure stable attention learning without overfitting.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the single linear feature projection head (64-dim) with a Multi-Head Cross-Attention mechanism: a 4-head attention layer that projects the 768-dim [CLS] token (as query) and engineered features (as key/value) into a shared 128-dim space before fusion. After cross-attention, concatenate the attention output with the original [CLS] token (768+128=896-dim) then pass through a 2-layer MLP with LayerNorm and dropout. This follows successful historical experiments where multi-head attention improved feature integration in author attribution tasks. The change directly addresses the signal dilution issue identified in the initial plan. Preserve all other model interfaces (input/output shapes, variable names).", "training_evaluation": "Implement curriculum label smoothing linearly decaying from 0.15 to 0.05 over the first 5 epochs, and use gradient accumulation (2 steps of batch size 16 → effective batch 32) to stabilize cross-attention gradient flow. Keep CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler but use a lower eta_min (1e-7) and adjust T_0 to restart every 2 epochs. This refinement avoids the failed OneCycleLR+high smoothing approach from historical experiments, instead using gradual annealing and gradient accumulation as validated by successful attention-based models. Preserve all other training parameters, data splitting, and inference ensemble strategy."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show validation log-loss plateauing around 0.41-0.43 with a training-validation gap of ~0.10, confirming the initial plan's diagnosis that engineered features are under-utilized. The initial plan's cross-attention mechanism and curriculum learning are promising but need refinement: historical success with multi-head attention on BiLSTM suggests attention works for author attribution, but failed experiments show OneCycleLR combined with high label smoothing can be unstable—here we use gradient accumulation and decaying label smoothing for stability. Model architecture change addresses the 64/832 dimension bottleneck directly, and training strategy changes ensure stable attention learning without overfitting.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the single linear feature projection head (64-dim) with a Multi-Head Cross-Attention mechanism: a 4-head attention layer that projects the 768-dim [CLS] token (as query) and engineered features (as key/value) into a shared 128-dim space before fusion. After cross-attention, concatenate the attention output with the original [CLS] token (768+128=896-dim) then pass through a 2-layer MLP with LayerNorm and dropout. This follows successful historical experiments where multi-head attention improved feature integration in author attribution tasks. The change directly addresses the signal dilution issue identified in the initial plan. Preserve all other model interfaces (input/output shapes, variable names).",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement curriculum label smoothing linearly decaying from 0.15 to 0.05 over the first 5 epochs, and use gradient accumulation (2 steps of batch size 16 → effective batch 32) to stabilize cross-attention gradient flow. Keep CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler but use a lower eta_min (1e-7) and adjust T_0 to restart every 2 epochs. This refinement avoids the failed OneCycleLR+high smoothing approach from historical experiments, instead using gradual annealing and gradient accumulation as validated by successful attention-based models. Preserve all other training parameters, data splitting, and inference ensemble strategy."
