{"reason": "The execution results show validation log-loss plateauing around 0.63-0.64 with early stopping at epoch 8, confirming the initial plan's diagnosis of underfitting due to excessive regularization (label smoothing 0.15, dropout 0.3) and a complex cross-attention mechanism that may degrade feature signal. Historical successful experiments show that direct feature concatenation with reduced regularization achieves better validation log-loss (0.50-0.55 range). Failed experiments with cross-attention suffered from unstable gradients and information bottleneck, reinforcing the initial plan's shift to direct concatenation. The model_design change to direct concatenation and training_evaluation adjustments (longer training, lower label smoothing, reduced dropout) directly address the underfitting issue. Data processing remains unchanged since the feature set is already comprehensive and per-fold vocabulary building is no longer needed with DistilBERT.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the multi-head cross-attention mechanism (query_proj, feature_proj, cross_attn, attention_pool) with direct concatenation of pooled engineered features (after a simple linear projection to 64-dim) with the [CLS] token embedding (768+64=832-dim). The feature projection is a single Linear(62, 64) layer with LayerNorm and GELU activation. The classifier input dimension changes to 832, maintaining the same 3-layer MLP topology. This simplifies gradient flow, eliminates the risk of attention distorting feature signal, and matches successful historical approaches that used direct concatenation for this task. All other model interfaces (input signatures, variable names) remain unchanged.", "training_evaluation": "Increase epochs to 25, decrease label smoothing from 0.15 to 0.06, and reduce classifier dropout from 0.3 to 0.2. Add a separate linear warmup scheduler (10% of steps) followed by cosine decay to 0 for the optimizer groups. Keep early stopping patience at 4. These changes are directly informed by the initial plan's rationale and historical success with milder regularization on this dataset. The lower label smoothing allows higher confidence predictions appropriate for a 3-class well-separated problem, while reduced dropout enables better signal propagation from the simplified feature integration. All other training parameters (differential LR, batch size, gradient clipping, AMP) remain unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show validation log-loss plateauing around 0.63-0.64 with early stopping at epoch 8, confirming the initial plan's diagnosis of underfitting due to excessive regularization (label smoothing 0.15, dropout 0.3) and a complex cross-attention mechanism that may degrade feature signal. Historical successful experiments show that direct feature concatenation with reduced regularization achieves better validation log-loss (0.50-0.55 range). Failed experiments with cross-attention suffered from unstable gradients and information bottleneck, reinforcing the initial plan's shift to direct concatenation. The model_design change to direct concatenation and training_evaluation adjustments (longer training, lower label smoothing, reduced dropout) directly address the underfitting issue. Data processing remains unchanged since the feature set is already comprehensive and per-fold vocabulary building is no longer needed with DistilBERT.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the multi-head cross-attention mechanism (query_proj, feature_proj, cross_attn, attention_pool) with direct concatenation of pooled engineered features (after a simple linear projection to 64-dim) with the [CLS] token embedding (768+64=832-dim). The feature projection is a single Linear(62, 64) layer with LayerNorm and GELU activation. The classifier input dimension changes to 832, maintaining the same 3-layer MLP topology. This simplifies gradient flow, eliminates the risk of attention distorting feature signal, and matches successful historical approaches that used direct concatenation for this task. All other model interfaces (input signatures, variable names) remain unchanged.",
    "training_evaluation": "Increase epochs to 25, decrease label smoothing from 0.15 to 0.06, and reduce classifier dropout from 0.3 to 0.2. Add a separate linear warmup scheduler (10% of steps) followed by cosine decay to 0 for the optimizer groups. Keep early stopping patience at 4. These changes are directly informed by the initial plan's rationale and historical success with milder regularization on this dataset. The lower label smoothing allows higher confidence predictions appropriate for a 3-class well-separated problem, while reduced dropout enables better signal propagation from the simplified feature integration. All other training parameters (differential LR, batch size, gradient clipping, AMP) remain unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan's core idea is to replace simple concatenation with cross-attention and augment feature/text interplay. Historical successful experiments confirm that multi-head cross-attention improves feature integration (val log loss dropped from 0.47 to 0.33 in one case). Failed experiments show OneCycleLR with high label smoothing can be unstable and that greedy MLP stacking overfits; instead, I adopt gradient accumulation and curriculum label smoothing (as in successful cross-attention experiments) to stabilize training and avoid overfitting. The initial plan's multi-head cross-attention (text tokens as keys/values, features as query) is preserved, but to reduce computational cost and memory, I scale down to 2 heads (from 4) and use 128-dim projection per head (as validated in successful experiments). For training, I adopt 3-fold CV (instead of 5) to balance time and variance reduction, and use gradient accumulation (effective batch 32) with curriculum label smoothing (decay from 0.15 to 0.05) to stabilize attention gradients. These refinements directly address the identified plateau (val loss 0.3355) while respecting historical lessons.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the simple concatenation of CLS token and 64-dim feature projection with a Multi-Head Cross-Attention mechanism: use the 768-dim CLS token as query, projected engineered features (via a 128-dim per head projection) as key and value, with 2 attention heads. After cross-attention, concatenate the attention output with the original CLS token (768+128=896 dim) and pass through a 2-layer MLP (896→256→3) with LayerNorm and dropout. This follows successful historical experiments where cross-attention improved feature-text fusion; the 2-head design reduces memory while retaining benefit. Preserve all existing variable names and model input/output signatures.", "training_evaluation": "Switch to 3-fold Stratified Cross-Validation (instead of single holdout) to reduce variance. Use gradient accumulation with 2 steps (effective batch size 32) to stabilize cross-attention gradients. Implement curriculum label smoothing that linearly decays from 0.15 to 0.05 over the first 5 epochs. Keep CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with T_0 restart every 2 epochs and eta_min=1e-7. Train each fold for up to 20 epochs with early stopping (patience 5), and average test predictions across folds. These changes are informed by successful experiments: 3-fold CV reduced variance without significant time cost, curriculum smoothing avoided the instability observed with fixed high smoothing, and gradient accumulation ensured stable attention learning."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's core idea is to replace simple concatenation with cross-attention and augment feature/text interplay. Historical successful experiments confirm that multi-head cross-attention improves feature integration (val log loss dropped from 0.47 to 0.33 in one case). Failed experiments show OneCycleLR with high label smoothing can be unstable and that greedy MLP stacking overfits; instead, I adopt gradient accumulation and curriculum label smoothing (as in successful cross-attention experiments) to stabilize training and avoid overfitting. The initial plan's multi-head cross-attention (text tokens as keys/values, features as query) is preserved, but to reduce computational cost and memory, I scale down to 2 heads (from 4) and use 128-dim projection per head (as validated in successful experiments). For training, I adopt 3-fold CV (instead of 5) to balance time and variance reduction, and use gradient accumulation (effective batch 32) with curriculum label smoothing (decay from 0.15 to 0.05) to stabilize attention gradients. These refinements directly address the identified plateau (val loss 0.3355) while respecting historical lessons.",
  "module": [
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the simple concatenation of CLS token and 64-dim feature projection with a Multi-Head Cross-Attention mechanism: use the 768-dim CLS token as query, projected engineered features (via a 128-dim per head projection) as key and value, with 2 attention heads. After cross-attention, concatenate the attention output with the original CLS token (768+128=896 dim) and pass through a 2-layer MLP (896→256→3) with LayerNorm and dropout. This follows successful historical experiments where cross-attention improved feature-text fusion; the 2-head design reduces memory while retaining benefit. Preserve all existing variable names and model input/output signatures.",
    "training_evaluation": "Switch to 3-fold Stratified Cross-Validation (instead of single holdout) to reduce variance. Use gradient accumulation with 2 steps (effective batch size 32) to stabilize cross-attention gradients. Implement curriculum label smoothing that linearly decays from 0.15 to 0.05 over the first 5 epochs. Keep CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with T_0 restart every 2 epochs and eta_min=1e-7. Train each fold for up to 20 epochs with early stopping (patience 5), and average test predictions across folds. These changes are informed by successful experiments: 3-fold CV reduced variance without significant time cost, curriculum smoothing avoided the instability observed with fixed high smoothing, and gradient accumulation ensured stable attention learning."
  }
}"}

